1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,180
Then there were the photographs.

2
00:00:01,820 --> 00:00:06,760
A young intelligence officer on the
British airborne corps staff, Major Brian

3
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:11,900
Urquhart, no relation to the general, kept
hearing from the Dutch resistance that

4
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:14,320
there was German armor around Arnhem.

5
00:00:14,910 --> 00:00:16,750
He asked for reconnaissance flights.

6
00:00:17,790 --> 00:00:22,370
On the 12th of September, a photo
reconnaissance Spitfire came back with

7
00:00:22,370 --> 00:00:24,870
pictures that showed tanks in the Arnhem
area.

8
00:00:25,170 --> 00:00:27,350
Erkhart took them to his commander.

9
00:00:28,050 --> 00:00:30,030
Lieutenant General Frederick Browning.

10
00:00:30,390 --> 00:00:31,930
Browning was dismissive.

11
00:00:32,410 --> 00:00:37,530
Soon afterwards, the core medical officer
was sent to see Major Erkhart, and Erkhart

12
00:00:37,530 --> 00:00:39,170
was sent home on sick leave.

13
00:00:39,630 --> 00:00:43,030
That's the story everyone knows, the
photographs that were ignored.

14
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:46,340
So let's check the second myth on the
road.

15
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:49,140
Were there really tanks waiting at Arnhem?

16
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:51,620
Sir, the pictures had tanks in them.

17
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:52,800
Pictures do.

18
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:55,100
Let's count what could actually drive.

19
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:56,120
barely any.

20
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:59,000
Most of the SS armor had been left in
Normandy.
